What are logs?
A log is a record of events that happen inside a computer or application.

These are examples of application log messages.
Common log levels:
Level	 Meaning
DEBUG	 Detailed debugging information
INFO	 Normal events
WARNING	 Something unusual happened
ERROR	 Something failed
CRITICAL A serious failure

example: If an application crashes, logs can reveal whether the cause was a database connection, missing configuration, permission problem, or insufficient memory.

Where logs are stored in ?
Linux commonly stores system logs in: /var/log/

Log file	         Purpose
/var/log/syslog	        system messages on Ubuntu
/var/log/auth.log	Authentication and login events
/var/log/kern.log	Kernel messages
/var/log/dpkg.log	Package installation activity
/var/log/apt/	        APT package manager logs
/var/log/nginx/	        Nginx logs, if configured

sudo cat /var/log/syslog - This displays the contents of the file.
sudo tail /var/log/syslog - Display the last 10 lines
sudo tail -n 50 /var/log/syslog - sudo tail -n 50 /var/log/syslog
sudo tail -f /var/log/syslog - Monitor logs continuously
sudo grep -i error /var/log/syslog - Search for errors

